  'history.label': 'Real History · {when}',
  'history.before': 'The real story · {year}',
  'history.after': 'What happened next · {year}',
  'history.side': 'Also in {year}',
  'history.failure': 'A failure story · {year}',
  'history.learnMore': 'Learn more →',
  'history.learnMoreName': 'Opens an official NASA page in a new tab',
  'history.continue': 'Continue',
  'history.explorer1Before.title': 'The race to space',
  'history.explorer1Before.text':
    "On 4 October 1957 the Soviet Union launched Sputnik, the first satellite. America's first try, Vanguard TV3, lost thrust and exploded on the pad on 6 December. Then the US Army's rocket team and JPL built Explorer 1 and readied its rocket in just 84 days. It carried a cosmic-ray detector and ran on batteries. Now it's your turn.",
  'history.explorer1Before.caption': 'Explorer 1 lifts off on its Juno I rocket from Cape Canaveral, 31 January 1958.',
  'history.explorer1Before.credit': 'Photo: NASA/MSFC',
  'history.explorer1After.title': 'The Van Allen belts',
  'history.explorer1After.text':
    'Explorer 1 reached orbit on 31 January 1958, before NASA even existed. Its detector counted far fewer cosmic rays than expected. James Van Allen worked out why: so many charged particles hit it that it was swamped. Earth is wrapped in belts of particles held by its magnetic field, now called the Van Allen belts. It was America\'s first discovery in space. Explorer 1 sent Data until its batteries ran out in May 1958, and NASA began on 1 October that year.',
  'history.explorer1After.caption': "JPL's William Pickering, James Van Allen and Wernher von Braun hold up a full-size model of Explorer 1 the night it reached orbit.",
  'history.explorer1After.credit': 'Photo: NASA/MSFC',
  'history.vanguard1.title': 'Power from the Sun',
  'history.vanguard1.text':
    "On 17 March 1958 the Navy's Vanguard 1 reached orbit. It was a ball just 15 cm across and the first satellite powered by sunlight. Its battery radio soon went quiet, but its solar-powered radio kept sending until May 1964. It is still up there, the oldest human-made object in orbit. Solar Cells are now unlocked in the Mission Designer.",
  'history.vanguard1.caption': 'One of the Vanguard satellites is checked at Cape Canaveral, Florida, in 1958.',
  'history.vanguard1.credit': 'Photo: NASA',
  'history.tiros1Before.title': 'Watching the weather',
  'history.tiros1Before.text':
    "Nobody had ever watched Earth's weather from space. NASA asked RCA to build TIROS-1: a spinning satellite with two small TV cameras to photograph the clouds. It weighed far more than Explorer 1, so it needed a bigger rocket, the Thor-Able, and power from about 9,000 solar cells charging its batteries. Now it's your turn.",
  'history.tiros1Before.caption': 'A Thor-Able lifts off from Cape Canaveral in August 1959 with Explorer 6. TIROS-1 flew on the same kind of rocket.',
  'history.tiros1Before.credit': 'Photo: NASA/MSFC',
  'history.tiros1After.title': 'The first weather satellite',
  'history.tiros1After.text':
    'TIROS-1 launched on 1 April 1960 and sent its first TV pictures of the clouds that same day. It worked for 78 days and sent home about 23,000 pictures, around 19,000 of them good enough to study the weather, until a power failure in June. It showed that satellites could watch the weather over the whole world, and they have done it ever since.',
  'history.tiros1After.caption': "Hurricane Gonzalo seen by NASA's Terra satellite in October 2014.",
  'history.tiros1After.credit': 'Image: NASA Goddard MODIS Rapid Response',
  'history.vanguardTV3.title': 'Up 1.2 metres, then down',
  'history.vanguardTV3.text':
    "America's first try at a satellite was Vanguard TV3. On 6 December 1957, with the world watching, it rose about 1.2 metres, lost thrust, fell back and exploded on the pad. Its small satellite was thrown clear, still sending its signal. Engineers traced the fault to the engine and fixed it, and it never failed that way again. On 17 March 1958 Vanguard 1 reached orbit. Every space team fails: find out why, fix it, and fly again.",
  'history.vanguardTV3.caption': 'A Vanguard rocket on its pad at Cape Canaveral in March 1958, before it launched Vanguard 1.',
  'history.vanguardTV3.credit': 'Photo: NASA',
  'history.telstar1Before.title': 'TV across the ocean',
  'history.telstar1Before.text':
    "In 1962 nobody could watch live TV from across the Atlantic: the Earth's curve blocks the signal. The phone company AT&T had Bell Labs build Telstar, a ball of solar cells just bigger than a beach ball, to catch TV signals from one side and send them down to the other. AT&T paid NASA for the rocket, so Telstar was the first satellite paid for by a company. On the ground, AT&T built a huge antenna in Andover, Maine. Now it's your turn.",
  'history.telstar1Before.caption': 'Telstar 1 lifts off on a Thor-Delta from Cape Canaveral, 10 July 1962.',
  'history.telstar1Before.credit': 'Photo: NASA',
  'history.telstar1After.title': 'Live via satellite',
  'history.telstar1After.text':
    "Telstar launched on 10 July 1962, and that same day it sent the first live TV pictures to France: an American flag waving outside the Andover Ground Station. Soon it carried TV, phone calls and pictures between America and Europe. But each pass over the Atlantic lasted only about 18 minutes, so the giant antennas had to chase it across the sky. In November radiation from a nuclear test high above the Pacific damaged it. A satellite that hung still over one spot would be far better.",
  'history.telstar1After.caption': "The horn antenna of AT&T's Ground Station at Andover, Maine: seven storeys high and weighing 340 tons, it tracked Telstar across the sky.",
  'history.telstar1After.credit': 'Photo: NASA',
  'history.syncom3Before.title': 'A satellite that stands still',
  'history.syncom3Before.text':
    'In 1945 the writer Arthur C. Clarke had an idea: a satellite 35,786 km up takes exactly a day to go round, so seen from Earth it hangs still in the sky, and dishes never have to chase it. Hughes built the Syncom satellites for NASA to try it. A rocket left each one in a long, stretched orbit; at its highest point the satellite fired its own engine to stay up there. Syncom 1 fell silent, Syncom 2 worked, and Syncom 3 would sit right over the equator. Now it is your turn.',
  'history.syncom3Before.caption': 'A Syncom satellite firing its own engine to reach its high orbit.',
  'history.syncom3Before.credit': 'Image: NASA',
  'history.syncom3After.title': 'The Tokyo Olympics, live',
  'history.syncom3After.text':
    'Syncom 3 launched on 19 August 1964 on a Delta D and became the first satellite to stand still over the equator. In October it carried live TV of the Olympic Games in Tokyo across the Pacific. The next year, on 6 April 1965, another Delta D launched Early Bird, the first commercial communications satellite. Today many weather and TV satellites sit in this same orbit.',
  'history.syncom3After.caption': 'A Delta rocket launches Intelsat III, one of the communications satellites that followed Syncom, 21 May 1969.',
  'history.syncom3After.credit': 'Photo: NASA/MSFC',

